# =============================================================================
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. `.env.local` is git-ignored; this file is
# committed and MUST NEVER CONTAIN A REAL VALUE.
#
# Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> API is where all three live.
# =============================================================================


# ---- SENT TO THE BROWSER -----------------------------------------------------
# Both of these are compiled into the client bundle. That is correct and safe:
# the publishable key can do NOTHING that Row Level Security does not allow, and
# every table in this project has RLS on with explicit policies (see
# supabase/migrations/). Publishing it is how the browser talks to Supabase at
# all.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...


# ---- SERVER ONLY. NEVER SENT TO THE BROWSER ----------------------------------
# =============================================================================
# 🔴 THIS KEY BYPASSES ROW LEVEL SECURITY ENTIRELY. It can read and write every
# row in the database. Anyone who obtains it owns the data.
#
# It is used by exactly three server actions, for the one thing RLS cannot
# express — creating and deleting rows in `auth.users`, which Supabase owns:
#
#   inviteAgentAccount        create an account, email a setup link
#   createAgentWithPassword   create an account with an admin-chosen password
#   deleteUnverifiedAccount   remove a squatted, never-confirmed address
#
# Every one of them calls `requireAdmin()` BEFORE the privileged client is
# constructed. CMS content, activity logs and page reads do not use it at all —
# they run under RLS as the logged-in user.
#
# FOUR THINGS KEEP IT OUT OF THE BROWSER, AND ALL FOUR MATTER:
#   1. NO `NEXT_PUBLIC_` PREFIX. This is the actual mechanism — Next only inlines
#      prefixed variables into the client bundle. Adding the prefix to this name
#      would publish the key on the next deploy.
#   2. `lib/supabase/admin.ts` throws if it is ever evaluated in a browser.
#   3. `scripts/check-no-service-role.mjs` runs on `postbuild` and FAILS THE
#      BUILD if `sb_secret_`, `service_role`, or either secret variable NAME
#      appears anywhere under the static output.
#   4. It is set in Vercel's environment variables, not in any committed file.
#
# On Vercel: Project -> Settings -> Environment Variables, scoped to
# Production/Preview. Do not paste it into a client component, a `NEXT_PUBLIC_`
# variable, or a support ticket.
# =============================================================================
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# The newer name for the same credential. `lib/supabase/admin.ts` accepts either;
# set ONE of the two, not both.
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=


# ---- OPTIONAL ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The canonical origin used to build password-setup links. Vercel provides
# VERCEL_URL automatically; set this only when that is wrong (a custom domain, or
# local development against a tunnel). See lib/site-origin.ts.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.fflsynergy.com

# Build output directory. Set to `.next-build` when producing a production build
# while `next dev` is running, so the two do not corrupt each other's manifests.
# NEXT_DIST_DIR=.next-build
